# Code owners (#195).
#
# Files where a mistake moves money or weakens an auth boundary get explicit,
# non-optional review from the facilitator maintainers. These are the ones a
# reviewer must actually read, not skim:
#
#   - src/facilitator.js — the scheme wiring, including which signer serves
#     which network (its own comments explain how a testnet key ends up
#     submitting to pubnet when signers are shared across networks).
#   - src/config.js — boot-time validation meant to make a misconfiguration
#     fail at start, including the pubnet opt-in.
#   - src/app.js — the auth middleware and every route.
#   - docs/THREAT-MODEL.md and SECURITY.md — what an external reviewer reads to
#     decide what is claimed.
#   - .github/workflows/** — a workflow diff can disable a gate, and it is the
#     diff reviewers skim hardest.
src/facilitator.js           @accensa/facilitator-maintainers
src/config.js                @accensa/facilitator-maintainers
src/app.js                   @accensa/facilitator-maintainers
docs/THREAT-MODEL.md         @accensa/facilitator-maintainers
SECURITY.md                  @accensa/facilitator-maintainers
.github/workflows/**         @accensa/facilitator-maintainers

# Catch-all. Per the team decision in #195 the same roster owns the rest, so a
# docs-only PR still waits on the team. Trade-off, stated plainly: a catch-all
# that owns every file makes each PR wait on one group and can get routed
# around; if that happens, loosen this to a permissive default (no owner) and
# keep the explicit sensitive-path rules above.
*                            @accensa/facilitator-maintainers
